"""Original first-year homeowner organization manuscript; independent case arithmetic."""
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))

chapter('Define the first-year ownership plan','Make the new responsibilities visible before buying upgrades.',[
'List the outcomes the household needs during the first year: usable rooms, reliable provider contacts, organized records and a realistic funding routine. Separate those outcomes from optional decoration and equipment wishes. Ownership creates ongoing decisions that are different from the purchase transaction.',
'Record what is known, what is supported by a document and what still needs assessment. A seller statement, an inspection observation and a professional repair finding are different evidence. Keep the source attached to each important assumption instead of turning an informal assurance into a fact.',
'Agree who manages records, books service, approves spending and follows up open issues. If more than one person shares those duties, choose a current coordinator and a backup. The plan should still work when one household member is unavailable.',
'Use the first year to build a reliable baseline rather than trying to complete every possible improvement immediately. Qualified providers must assess technical condition and urgency. Review priorities when actual findings or household needs change.'
], 'A fictional household has ten first-year goals: four immediate organization needs, three assessed follow-ups and three optional improvements.', [('Organization needs','4 goals'),('Assessed follow-ups','3 goals'),('Optional improvements','3 goals'),('Total first-year list','10 goals')],
'The categories organize decisions. They do not assign a safe delay or repair priority to an actual condition.',
['First-year household outcomes / occupied needs','Known facts / documents / unverified assumptions','Organization / assessed follow-up / optional choices','Coordinator / backup / booking and spending authority','Review dates / provider findings / changed priorities'],
['Evidence sources remain attached.','Technical urgency is not guessed.','Ownership duties have accountable people.'], 'Agree on a short first-year outcome list before making a large optional purchase.')

chapter('Collect the handover file','A useful home record begins with the documents already available.',[
'Gather the inspection report, seller-provided records, relevant drawings, warranties and service invoices available to the owner. Save a current copy in an indexed folder and retain original documents where appropriate. Mark missing records as unknown rather than reconstructing them from memory.',
'Distinguish what a document covers from what it does not establish. A past service invoice may identify a component and date but cannot guarantee present condition. An old drawing may not describe later alterations. Ask the responsible provider about important gaps.',
'Keep sensitive personal, account and transaction information separate from the technical file routinely shared with contractors. A bidder generally needs relevant project evidence, not every document about the household. Share only what the requested assessment requires.',
'Create a small document register with title, date, issuer, asset or room and current status. Use the register to request missing information from the appropriate source. Do not describe the file as complete merely because a large folder has been uploaded.'
], 'A fictional handover list expects ten relevant records. Seven are available, two are requested and one is confirmed unavailable.', [('Available records','7'),('Requested records','2'),('Unavailable record','1'),('Expected list','10 records')],
'Unavailable information remains an explicit uncertainty. It is not evidence that the underlying work was never done.',
['Relevant handover records / source / received date','Document scope / version / current relevance','Missing / requested / unavailable status','Sensitive information / permitted sharing boundary','Register owner / follow-up contact / next action'],
['Old records are not present-condition guarantees.','Missing documents are labeled honestly.','Contractor sharing excludes unnecessary private data.'], 'Create the document register and request the most decision-relevant missing records first.')

chapter('Map owner and shared-building responsibilities','The visible component may have a different maintenance owner.',[
'List the services, building elements and shared facilities affecting the home. Ask which responsibilities belong to the owner, an association, a managing agent, a utility or another party. Use current written governing or service documents and appropriate advice where the boundary is unclear.',
'Record both the duty to arrange work and the duty to pay. They may not belong to the same person or organization. A shared service fee does not automatically mean every repair to a connected component is included.',
'Keep approval and access requirements with the responsibility record. Work on an owner-managed item can still affect shared areas or require coordination. Do not treat physical access as permission to modify a component.',
'Resolve uncertainty before relying on another party to perform essential work. The worksheet organizes questions and evidence; it does not interpret property documents or decide legal liability. Retain the responsible contact and the actual clarification.'
], 'A fictional building has nine responsibility items: five owner-managed, two association-managed, one utility-managed and one unresolved.', [('Owner','5 items'),('Association','2 items'),('Utility','1 item'),('Unresolved','1 item')],
'The unresolved item needs a documented answer. It is not assumed included in a shared fee.',
['Component / service / responsibility question','Arrange-work owner / payment owner / evidence','Approval and shared-access requirements','Responsible contact / written clarification','Unresolved boundary / decision deadline / next action'],
['Work and payment responsibilities are distinct.','Shared fees are not assumed comprehensive.','Permission is verified before modification.'], 'Confirm the responsibility boundaries before booking work affecting shared systems or areas.')

chapter('Build an accessible asset inventory','Record what is installed without undertaking a technical inspection.',[
'List major equipment and relevant home components with a location and unique ID. Use accessible labels, existing documents and provider records to capture manufacturer, model and known installation information. Leave dates unknown when the evidence is unavailable.',
'Link manuals, warranty details and service contacts to the asset. Record whether the information describes the actual installed product or an earlier selection. A similar model name can lead to the wrong care requirements.',
'Keep observed condition and provider-assessed findings separate. An older asset is not automatically defective, and a newer one is not automatically serviceable. Ask qualified providers to resolve condition and safety questions relevant to the home.',
'Do not open equipment, climb into hazardous areas or disturb materials to fill the inventory. Ask a qualified provider to supply details during an appropriate visit. The register is a planning index, not a certification of the home.'
], 'A fictional first inventory has eight assets. Six have exact model references and two await provider-supplied information.', [('Assets listed','8'),('Exact model references','6'),('Information pending','2'),('Invented installation dates','0')],
'An incomplete but accurate inventory is more useful than a complete-looking one built from guesses.',
['Asset ID / normal accessible location','Manufacturer / model / source / installation evidence','Actual manual / warranty / provider links','Observation versus professional finding','Missing details / provider / safe next action'],
['Unknown dates stay unknown.','Documents match the installed product.','No hazardous information-gathering task is required.'], 'Start with accessible documents and ask service providers to fill the relevant information gaps.')

chapter('Create the provider and contact directory','Find the right contact before an issue becomes urgent.',[
'Record verified contacts for utilities, the building manager where relevant, routine service providers and appropriate local emergency information. Keep routine booking and urgent-response contacts distinct. Confirm current numbers through the provider or official source rather than relying on an old sticker.',
'Add the property or asset reference needed for a service request, while keeping credentials and sensitive account data in an appropriately protected place. A shared contact sheet does not need passwords, payment-card details or a complete personal file.',
'Assign a backup household contact and explain where the directory is available. Consider access when a phone or internet connection is unavailable. Ready.gov provides household planning resources that can support a separate emergency communication plan [2].',
'Review the directory after provider changes and record the verification date. This book does not dispatch help or supply technical emergency procedures. Use the actual local emergency or utility response when the situation requires it.'
], 'A fictional directory lists six providers. Four contacts are verified, one needs updating and one is still missing.', [('Verified contacts','4'),('Outdated contact','1'),('Missing contact','1'),('Ready for full reliance','No')],
'A name on the sheet is not the same as a verified route to the right service.',
['Routine / urgent / official contact categories','Provider names / current details / verified dates','Property or asset references needed for booking','Backup contact / offline access / privacy boundary','Missing details / updates / responsible owner'],
['Urgent and routine contacts are distinct.','The shared sheet contains no credentials.','Unverified contacts remain open tasks.'], 'Verify the essential contacts and make a usable copy available to the household.')

chapter('Organize household readiness and alarm records','Use official guidance and actual device instructions.',[
'Create a separate household readiness record covering communication, meeting arrangements and the needs of the people using the home. Ready.gov encourages documenting and practicing a household plan [2]. Use current local guidance for the actual circumstances.',
'Record installed alarm devices and link their actual manufacturer instructions and relevant local requirements. U.S. Fire Administration resources explain the importance of working smoke alarms and home-fire preparation [3]. Ask the appropriate qualified provider or local fire service about unresolved placement, condition or suitability questions.',
'Turn sourced device-care requirements into assigned tasks with dates or triggers. This book supplies no wiring, installation, testing or replacement procedure and no universal interval. A device listed in a workbook has not thereby been verified to work.',
'Keep readiness planning separate from optional improvement budgeting. If the household identifies an immediate danger or an urgent professional finding, use the appropriate response rather than waiting for the next scheduled planning review.'
], 'A fictional record has four alarm IDs. Three have matching instructions on file; one needs model identification and provider follow-up. No functional result is assumed.', [('Alarm records','4'),('Matching instructions','3'),('Information unresolved','1'),('Verified operating status','Not determined by this record')],
'Document counts do not certify alarm performance. Actual assessment and care must follow the appropriate guidance.',
['Household communication and meeting-plan reference','Alarm IDs / actual instructions / local questions','Qualified advice / unresolved condition or placement','Sourced care tasks / owner / next trigger','Readiness review / practice / urgent-response contacts'],
['A listed device is not labeled verified working.','Care tasks use actual sources.','Urgent matters do not wait for a budget cycle.'], 'Use official readiness resources and resolve device questions with the responsible qualified source.')

chapter('Turn inspection observations into an action queue','A report becomes useful when each item has a next step.',[
'Read the actual inspection report and separate observed issues, limitations and recommendations. Keep the report wording and location reference available when discussing an item with a provider. A general inspection may leave systems or areas outside its scope.',
'Assign each relevant item an action: obtain further assessment, request a defined proposal, seek a missing record or retain it for routine planning. Let qualified findings establish urgency and suitability. Do not diagnose a technical condition from a brief report heading.',
'Record the responsible person, contact and decision date. If a recommendation is unresolved, leave it open even when other work is completed nearby. A room looking improved does not prove that an underlying reported concern has been addressed.',
'Link assessed work to the funding plan and retain completion evidence. The queue should distinguish investigation cost, proposed work, approved work and paid invoices. That prevents a rough allowance from being mistaken for a resolved condition.'
], 'A fictional report produces twelve owner actions. Four are closed with evidence, five await provider responses and three await owner document requests.', [('Closed with evidence','4'),('Provider response pending','5'),('Owner request pending','3'),('Open actions','8')],
'Four closed actions do not certify the rest of the home. Review each item against its actual evidence.',
['Report item / location / limitation / recommendation','Next action / qualified assessor / contact','Responsible owner / due date / urgent guidance','Assessment / proposed / approved / paid status','Completion evidence / unresolved dependencies'],
['Report limitations remain visible.','Technical urgency comes from qualified guidance.','Closure requires evidence of the defined action.'], 'Give every relevant inspection recommendation a named next-action owner.')

chapter('Plan the first month of organization','Choose a realistic setup period without inventing maintenance deadlines.',[
'Use the first month as a household organization window for records, contacts and scheduling. This is a planning choice, not a universal deadline for all maintenance. Any urgent matter or required service must follow its actual guidance regardless of the chosen setup period.',
'Select a manageable number of tasks and assign a result, owner and date to each. Examples include obtaining a manual, verifying a contact or booking an assessment. A vague task such as sort the house is difficult to complete or review.',
'Separate administrative tasks from technical work. Gathering an invoice is different from opening equipment to identify a part. When information requires a qualified visit, book the provider rather than expanding an owner task beyond a sensible boundary.',
'Review what remains open and why. A task awaiting another party should retain its request date and follow-up. Carry unfinished matters into the active queue instead of treating the end of the month as completion.'
], 'An invented first-month plan has fifteen organization tasks. Ten are completed, three await external replies and two remain unstarted.', [('Completed','10'),('Awaiting replies','3'),('Not started','2'),('Still open','5')],
'The month is an organizing window. It does not determine a safe delay for a technical issue.',
['Setup tasks / specific completion results','Task owner / backup / chosen dates','Administrative versus qualified-provider work','External requests / follow-up / response evidence','Open items / revised plan / urgent exceptions'],
['Setup dates do not override actual requirements.','Tasks have concrete completion results.','External delays remain in the action queue.'], 'Choose a small first-month setup plan and carry every unfinished task into the current record.')

chapter('Build maintenance tasks from actual instructions','Use the home documents instead of a universal checklist.',[
'For each asset, identify the tasks and triggers in the current manual and relevant provider recommendations. Record the source, responsible person and evidence of completion. Some tasks depend on time, others on use or assessed conditions.',
'Ask which tasks are suitable for the owner and which need a qualified provider. This handbook gives no technical servicing procedure. A task should not be assigned to the household merely because it appears short or inexpensive.',
'Place the defined tasks into a calendar with enough booking and access lead time. Keep tentative appointments separate from completed service. If an interval or task is unclear, ask the source or provider rather than inventing an annual date.',
'Review the task after service and update its next trigger using the applicable source. Keep limitations and outstanding recommendations visible. The calendar coordinates care but does not certify asset condition or guarantee that failure will be prevented.'
], 'A fictional six-asset inventory produces nine sourced tasks: seven have defined triggers and two need provider clarification.', [('Assets','6'),('Sourced tasks','9'),('Defined triggers','7'),('Clarification pending','2')],
'The two open tasks remain on the question list rather than receiving guessed intervals.',
['Asset / task / exact source and version','Time / use / condition trigger and rationale','Owner-permitted or qualified-provider responsibility','Booking lead time / next action / completion evidence','Unclear requirements / provider questions / revisions'],
['Every interval has a source.','Appointments are not marked completed.','Unknown requirements remain visible.'], 'Build the first calendar from actual documents and confirm unclear tasks with the responsible provider.')

chapter('Review local seasons and periods away','The household calendar must fit the actual home.',[
'Ask relevant providers how local conditions, seasonal use and periods of absence affect the actual maintenance plan. Keep their requirements and recommendations with the appropriate asset or building element. A calendar copied from another climate may not fit the home.',
'Record booking lead times and household availability. Coordinate access for providers and identify a trusted contact where appropriate. Do not place private access credentials on a general maintenance worksheet shared with bidders.',
'Review how absence affects scheduled tasks, communications and provider arrangements. Obtain appropriate guidance for the actual systems. This book supplies no winterization, isolation, shutdown or restart procedure.',
'Keep the selected arrangements and review dates in the household plan. A missed task should trigger a next action and provider contact as relevant, rather than silently being moved to the next year.'
], 'A fictional calendar has four tasks affected by a planned absence. Two are rescheduled with providers, one has a confirmed alternate owner and one remains unresolved.', [('Rescheduled with confirmation','2'),('Alternate owner confirmed','1'),('Unresolved','1'),('Affected tasks','4')],
'The open task requires a decision. A period away does not automatically suspend its actual requirement.',
['Local-condition questions / provider sources','Seasonal or absence-related tasks / actual triggers','Bookings / access arrangements / privacy boundary','Alternate owners / communications / confirmations','Unresolved task / deadline / review on return'],
['The plan fits actual local conditions.','Private access details are protected.','Absence does not erase required tasks.'], 'Review upcoming absences with the relevant providers before moving task dates.')

chapter('Set up utility and bill records','Separate usage, rates and household changes.',[
'Record the utility or service provider, billing units, normal billing cycle and relevant contact. Keep account access and payment credentials in a protected location separate from contractor files. Save bills in an accessible period-by-period record.',
'When comparing periods, note the number of days and changes in occupancy, weather or use. A higher bill can reflect a rate change, fees or a longer billing period. Do not treat cost alone as a diagnosis of equipment performance or a water leak.',
'Create simple daily-average calculations where they help explain the record. Keep different units separate and ask the provider about unexplained billing questions. A normalized comparison can identify a question without proving its cause.',
'Use actual subsequent bills to improve the first-year household forecast. Retain estimated charges and adjustments with their status. An estimate is not the same as a final measured bill, and a requested correction is not a confirmed credit.'
], 'A fictional water record shows 4,500 gallons over 30 days and 4,200 gallons over 28 days. Each equals 150 gallons per day.', [('4,500 / 30','150 gallons per day'),('4,200 / 28','150 gallons per day'),('Raw volume difference','300 gallons'),('Daily-average difference','0 gallons')],
'Matching daily averages do not establish that every fixture is operating correctly. They simply improve the comparison.',
['Provider / billing units / days / contact','Bills / measured or estimated status / adjustments','Occupancy / weather / usage changes','Daily-average calculation / unexplained questions','Forecast update / confirmed credits / privacy boundary'],
['Period length is visible.','Price changes are separate from usage changes.','Comparisons do not become diagnoses.'], 'Keep the first bills with their units and period lengths before drawing conclusions from a total.')

chapter('Use water observations to request the right help','A clear report is more useful than an owner diagnosis.',[
'Record accessible observations such as a recurring drip, an unusual bill or a wet area with a date and location. Avoid disturbing materials or investigating equipment to improve the note. Ask the appropriate qualified provider to assess the condition and urgency.',
'Link the observation to the relevant asset and any prior records. State what changed and what remains unknown. Do not describe a stain as proof of a failed pipe or assume that all water-related concerns have the same cause.',
'Request a defined assessment or repair proposal and record what it addresses. Keep investigation, ordinary repair and restoration scope separate where the providers do. Record approved work and the evidence needed for follow-up.',
'Compare later observations without promising that one repair explains every change in use or cost. Household conditions may also change. Retain open questions rather than treating a paid invoice as automatic proof that the entire concern is resolved.'
], 'An invented assessed repair proposal is $220 with $80 separate restoration. The restoration is expressly excluded from the repair price.', [('Defined repair','$220'),('Separate restoration','$80'),('Complete stated scope','$300'),('Other concealed work','Not established')],
'Confirm that restoration is not included elsewhere. Actual technical scope and urgency require provider assessment.',
['Observation / date / location / safe evidence','Asset / prior reports / changed conditions','Qualified assessor / urgency / defined next action','Repair / restoration / included and excluded costs','Approved work / outcome evidence / follow-up date'],
['Observations stay distinct from causes.','Supporting restoration is visible.','Repair follow-up has an accountable owner.'], 'Provide the same dated observation record when requesting assessment and a complete proposal.')

chapter('Review energy questions before choosing upgrades','First-year bills are a baseline with limitations.',[
'Collect actual energy bills and record their units, period lengths and household context. Equipment labels or a seller recollection cannot establish future household consumption. Keep estimates and missing periods explicit.',
'Write the issue the household wants to address: cost, comfort, noise, recurring service or another outcome. Ask the appropriate qualified provider what assessment is needed. A new product with a better advertised rating does not by itself establish the benefit of the installed change.',
'Compare complete assessed alternatives, including supporting work and ongoing obligations. Keep future operating costs and savings as scenarios with stated assumptions. Do not use a short first-year bill history as proof of a guaranteed upgrade return.',
'Record which changes were made and compare later use carefully with relevant weather, occupancy and rate differences. The workbook organizes questions and arithmetic, not an energy audit, system design or causal savings analysis.'
], 'A fictional upgrade costs $2,400 and assumes $200 per year of net savings only to illustrate simple payback. The arithmetic ignores financing, time value and future changes.', [('Installed scenario cost','$2,400'),('Assumed net annual saving','$200'),('$2,400 / $200','12 years simple payback'),('Actual savings','Not established')],
'Twelve years is a scenario result, not a forecast or recommendation. A different saving assumption changes it directly.',
['Actual energy bills / units / context / limitations','Desired outcome / qualified assessment questions','Complete alternatives / supporting costs / obligations','Operating-cost assumptions / exclusions / scenario','Changes made / later comparison / evidence limits'],
['Advertised ratings are not household savings.','Payback assumptions remain visible.','The record does not claim causal proof.'], 'Define the energy or comfort question and request the relevant assessment before buying an upgrade.')

chapter('Separate warranties, service plans and insurance questions','Different documents answer different ownership concerns.',[
'Collect current product warranties, contractor warranties and any service-plan terms that apply. Record the named product, issuer, dates, exclusions and reporting process. Do not assume that a transferable document automatically applies to the new owner without checking its actual terms.',
'Keep service contracts separate from warranty promises and insurance policies. A scheduled-service plan may exclude repairs, and a repair-related promise may require a specific claims process. Ask the issuing provider or relevant adviser about uncertain coverage and obligations.',
'Record renewal dates, fees and cancellation terms before treating a recurring plan as part of the household routine. Compare the actual defined benefits with the alternative of separately priced service. Conditional discounts are not guaranteed savings.',
'Retain the answer and source for important questions. This handbook does not interpret insurance, warranty or contract rights or recommend a policy. It provides a record for obtaining and using the relevant current information.'
], 'A fictional service plan is $25 per month. Two matched visits would cost $130 each. No repair coverage or other benefit is valued in the narrow comparison.', [('12 × $25','$300 annual plan'),('2 × $130','$260 visit alternative'),('Plan premium','$40'),('Other benefits and coverage','Require actual term review')],
'A $40 difference does not determine the right option. Confirm the actual scope, reporting process and relevance to the home.',
['Product / contractor / service / insurance documents','Issuer / dates / applicability / reporting route','Exclusions / obligations / open coverage questions','Renewal / fee / cancellation / matched alternative','Confirmed answers / relevant adviser / retained evidence'],
['Different document types remain separate.','Transfer and coverage are checked.','Conditional benefits are not labeled assured savings.'], 'Ask the issuer to clarify applicability and the claims process before relying on a document.')

chapter('Build a first-year care spending plan','Recurring service and optional improvements need separate lines.',[
'List sourced routine tasks, assessed repairs and known professional visits with their current scope and cost status. Keep estimates, accepted proposals and paid bills distinct. The first-year budget should reflect the actual home rather than a universal percentage of purchase price.',
'Separate move-in organization purchases, routine care, assessed repairs and optional upgrades. A large decorative purchase should not quietly consume funds assigned to an accepted repair obligation. Record the chosen priorities and the household funding boundary.',
'Include relevant fixed fees, access, tax and restoration only where the actual terms require them. Reconcile overlap across packages and owner purchases. Unknown work should stay visible rather than being treated as zero.',
'Review the plan as assessments and actual invoices arrive. Explain differences by scope, quantity, timing or price. Keep the original baseline so the household can learn which first-year assumptions need improvement.'
], 'A fictional care plan has $500 routine service, $900 assessed repair and $200 organization purchases. A separate $700 optional upgrade is not yet approved.', [('Routine service','$500'),('Assessed repair','$900'),('Organization purchases','$200'),('Defined plan','$1,600'),('Optional unapproved choice','$700')],
'The $700 is a decision option, not yet an accepted obligation. Show its funding effect before approval.',
['Routine / repair / setup / optional categories','Sourced tasks / current proposals / cost status','Tax / access / fixed fees / restoration inclusions','Funding boundary / open costs / priority decisions','Actual invoices / forecast revisions / variance reasons'],
['The home-specific plan replaces a generic percentage.','Optional choices remain separate.','Unknown required work is not priced at zero.'], 'Build the first care budget from sourced tasks and assessed work before adding optional upgrades.')

chapter('Create a reserve scenario and payment calendar','Money set aside is different from money spent.',[
'Choose a clearly labeled reserve target using the household plan and actual information available. Record opening designated funds and a chosen saving period. A target and horizon are planning decisions, not predictions of when equipment will fail.',
'Calculate the unfunded gap and divide it by the selected number of months for a simple contribution scenario. State excluded effects such as interest, inflation or withdrawals. Do not count a transfer into savings as a service expense.',
'Map accepted bills to their due dates alongside available funds. An annual average can hide a payment due before the planned contributions accumulate. Keep unpaid obligations visible beside the cash balance.',
'Review the plan when an assessment, household constraint or accepted work changes. Urgent professional needs are separate from a long saving horizon. This worksheet provides arithmetic rather than a borrowing recommendation.'
], 'A fictional reserve target is $3,000, with $600 already designated and a chosen twelve-month contribution period. No interest or other withdrawals are modeled.', [('Target','$3,000'),('Opening designated balance','−$600'),('Gap','$2,400'),('$2,400 / 12','$200 per month')],
'The twelve months is a saving choice, not an equipment-life estimate or permission to delay required work.',
['Target rationale / actual information / uncertainty','Opening funds / other claims / selected horizon','Gap / contribution / excluded model effects','Bill due dates / available cash / unpaid obligations','Review triggers / urgent needs / revised funding decision'],
['Reserve transfers are not duplicated as expenses.','Saving horizon is not remaining asset life.','Early due dates are checked against actual cash.'], 'Compare contribution timing with the first accepted bills before relying on a monthly average.')

chapter('Request a complete first contractor proposal','A clear question helps a new owner compare useful answers.',[
'Describe the observed concern or desired outcome using the current asset and room records. Provide relevant documents and label unverified assumptions. Ask for an assessed scope rather than demanding a particular repair you have diagnosed from an online search.',
'Request included and excluded tasks, exact selected products where relevant, allowances, complete price, timing and payment terms. FTC contractor guidance supports checking providers and reviewing written estimates and agreements [1]. Use appropriate local sources to verify relevant current requirements.',
'Compare the same scope across providers and clarify gaps in writing. A lower headline may exclude access, restoration or a required supporting service. Keep unresolved technical and regulatory questions with the appropriate qualified source.',
'Retain the accepted proposal and change process before work begins. The workbook does not provide a contract template or certify provider competence. It gives a new owner a practical record of the questions, evidence and decision.'
], 'Fictional bid A is $1,000 excluding $250 confirmed restoration. Bid B is $1,200 including the matched restoration. No other differences are assumed.', [('A plus required restoration','$1,250'),('Matched bid B','$1,200'),('Defined-scope difference','$50 in B’s favor'),('Suitability and terms','Still require review')],
'The lower headline is not the lower matched total. Do not invent a restoration price if no assessed quote exists.',
['Observation or outcome / room / asset / documents','Assessed scope / inclusions / exclusions / allowances','Complete price / schedule / payment and change terms','Provider verification / local questions / written clarifications','Matched comparison / accepted version / rationale'],
['Owner observations are not repair diagnoses.','Required supporting work is included.','The selected proposal has written evidence.'], 'Ask for the full assessed scope and compare required excluded work before choosing a provider.')

chapter('Keep repair and service history from the start','A paid bill needs a clear description of completed work.',[
'Record the asset or room, date, provider, defined task and outcome for each event. Link the accepted scope, approved changes, service report and invoice. A future provider should be able to understand what was actually done without reconstructing a long message history.',
'Keep proposed, approved, completed, invoiced and paid statuses distinct. A quote is not a debt, and a paid invoice does not by itself prove every related concern is resolved. Record required evidence and the responsible provider follow-up.',
'Update product details and care tasks when work changes the installed equipment or materials. Retain old records as history while making the current version clear. A replaced model manual should not remain the active instruction source.',
'Reconcile confirmed credits and unpaid obligations in the cost record. Keep requested credits pending until supported by evidence. This practice gives the first annual review a reliable record instead of a pile of unrelated receipts.'
], 'A fictional repair has $900 accepted scope and a $100 approved addition. Payments total $700, with no confirmed credit.', [('Accepted scope plus change','$1,000'),('Paid','$700'),('Remaining obligation','$300'),('Requested but unconfirmed credit','Not deducted')],
'The $300 is unpaid defined cost. Completion status is checked separately against actual work evidence.',
['Room or asset / date / provider / accepted task','Proposed / approved / complete / invoice / paid status','Reports / outcome / open recommendations','Installed-product changes / current care instructions','Final cost / payments / confirmed credits / obligations'],
['Status labels remain distinct.','Current instructions match current products.','Only confirmed credits reduce the forecast.'], 'Save a complete event record after the first service visit and use the same format thereafter.')

chapter('Review the first year and set the next baseline','Use actual evidence to make the second year easier.',[
'Review the first-year outcomes, open action queue, service history and current asset records. Identify which missing documents were resolved and which still matter. A large number of completed administrative tasks does not certify every system condition.',
'Compare actual cost and unpaid obligations with the original care plan. Separate routine expenses, assessed repairs, setup purchases, optional improvements and reserve transfers. Explain the causes of differences before revising next-year assumptions.',
'Update the calendar from actual manuals, qualified findings and household use. Retain unresolved technical questions and relevant contacts. Do not automatically repeat the first-year setup purchases or advance every task by one year.',
'Agree who maintains the record and when it will next be reviewed. Keep private data protected and current provider information accessible. The result should be a usable ownership routine, with limits and open decisions honestly recorded.'
], 'A fictional year paid $1,450 of care expenses and has $150 confirmed unpaid cost, matching the original $1,600 defined plan. Separate reserve contributions are excluded.', [('Paid care cost','$1,450'),('Unpaid confirmed cost','$150'),('Total care expense','$1,600'),('Variance to $1,600 plan','$0')],
'A $150 difference in cash paid is not a saving when the $150 obligation remains due.',
['First-year outcomes / open actions / missing records','Actual costs / unpaid obligations / category totals','Baseline comparison / variance causes / reserve transfers','Updated sourced calendar / current providers / open findings','Record owner / next review / second-year assumptions'],
['Cost includes unpaid confirmed obligations.','Setup and routine expenses stay distinct.','The next plan uses current evidence.'], 'Close the first year with one reconciled cost record and a sourced next-action calendar.')

SOURCES=[('1','FTC: Home Repair Scams','https://consumer.ftc.gov/features/pass-it-on/home-repair-scams'),('2','Ready.gov: Make a Plan','https://www.ready.gov/plan'),('3','U.S. Fire Administration: Smoke Alarms','https://www.usfa.fema.gov/prevention/home-fires/prepare-for-fire/smoke-alarms/')]

